            <ArticleTitle>Isolated atresia of the left main coronary artery in an adult.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>169-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 49-year-old woman with isolated atresia of the left main coronary artery is presented. The patient who suffered from angina underwent myocardial revascularization 14 years after the onset of symptoms. A left internal mammary artery to left anterior descending coronary artery bypass was performed. Twenty months after the operation, the patient is free from angina and leads a normal life. The clinical features, diagnosis and management of the left main coronary atresia in adults are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
